#uuid "02305ea5-f68b-5c83-8300-962012453c58" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186178663 #edb/ref 1080 "3i - 4j - 4k" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186178663 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186178663 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "08eab681-1715-57de-94d8-fe843e62627f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186178664 #edb/ref 1080 "2i - 3j - 2k" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186178664 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186178664 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "3c7e4830-878c-5872-8b1e-42a8a24d5185" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186178665 #edb/ref 1080 "3i - 3j - 2k" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186178665 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186178665 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "68cbb0e0-e3b9-565c-ab91-74bc063eff39" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179548 #edb/ref 1069 "e-8745" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179548 #edb/ref 1071 "Calculate $\\nabla\\times(3\\mathbf{F} + \\mathbf{G})$ at the point $(-1,1,0)$ for the vector fields $\\mathbf F$ and $\\mathbf G,$ where\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z) = 3x\\,\\mathbf{i} + y^2z\\,\\mathbf{j} + 2\\,\\mathbf{k}, \\quad (\\nabla\\times\\mathbf{G})(x,y,z) = 3y^2\\,\\mathbf{i} + 4z\\,\\mathbf{j} + \\mathbf{k}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179548 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "3a28f359-71aa-5b9b-b55c-802374e6821f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179548 #edb/ref 1074 "If $\\mathbf F$ and $\\mathbf G$ are vector fields and $k$ is a constant, then we have the following linearity properties for curl:\n\n- $\\nabla \\times (k \\mathbf F) = k \\nabla \\times \\mathbf F$\n\n- $\\nabla \\times (\\mathbf F + \\mathbf G) = \\nabla \\times \\mathbf F + \\nabla \\times \\mathbf G$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\times (3F+G) & =∇\\times (3F)+∇\\times G \\\\  & =3∇\\times F+∇\\times G.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to calculate $\\nabla \\times \\mathbf{F}.$ In doing so, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\times F & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x} & \\frac{∂}{∂y} & \\frac{∂}{∂z} \\\\ 3x & {y}^{2}z & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =[\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2)-\\frac{∂}{∂z}({y}^{2}z)]i-[\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2)-\\frac{∂}{∂z}(3x)]j+[\\frac{∂}{∂x}({y}^{2}z)-\\frac{∂}{∂y}(3x)]k \\\\  & =(0-{y}^{2})i-(0-0)j+(0-0)k \\\\  & =-{y}^{2}\\,i.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the linearity properties, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\times (3F+G) & =3∇\\times F+∇\\times G \\\\  & =3[-{y}^{2}\\,i]+[3{y}^{2}\\,i+4z\\,j+k] \\\\  & =-3{y}^{2}\\,i+3{y}^{2}\\,i+4z\\,j+k \\\\  & =(3{y}^{2}-3{y}^{2})\\,i+4z\\,j+k \\\\  & =4z\\,j+k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating at the point $(-1,1,0),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(∇\\times (F-G))(-1,1,0) & =4(0)\\,j+k=k.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179550 #edb/ref 1069 "e-8931" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179550 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf F(x,y,z) =  \\langle 0, y-z, x-y\\rangle$ and the scalar field $f(x,y,z) = x+y.$ Given that\n\n$$\n\\nabla\\times \\mathbf F = \\langle 0,\\,-1,\\,0\\rangle, \n\\quad\n\\nabla f = \\langle 1,\\, 1,\\, 0\\rangle,\n$$\n\ncalculate $\\nabla\\times(f\\,\\mathbf F)$ at the point $(-1,1,3).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179550 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e27e6735-4d50-5c26-a20d-44454ddcdef3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179550 #edb/ref 1074 "We shall use the following identity:\n\n$$\n\\nabla\\times(f\\,\\mathbf F) = f \\nabla \\times \\mathbf {F}+   \\nabla f \\times \\mathbf F\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute $\\nabla f \\times \\mathbf F.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f\\times F & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & y-z & x-y\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =[1\\cdot (x-y)-0\\cdot (y-z)]\\,i-[1\\cdot (x-y)-0\\cdot (0)]\\,j+[1\\cdot (y-z)-1\\cdot (0)]\\,k \\\\  & =(x-y)\\,i+(y-x)\\,j+(y-z)\\,k \\\\  & =⟨x-y,\\,y-x,\\,y-z⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\times (f\\,F) & =f∇\\times F+∇f\\times F \\\\  & =(x+y)⟨0,-1,0⟩+⟨x-y,\\,y-x,\\,y-z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,-(x+y),0⟩+⟨x-y,\\,y-x,\\,y-z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨x-y,\\,-2x,\\,y-z⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\times (f\\,F)(-1,1,3) & =⟨(-1)-1,\\,-2(-1),\\,1-3⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-2,\\,2,\\,-2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179689 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048932 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179689 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186051085 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179689 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186179911 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179689 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186180186 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179689 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186241125 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179689 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186241126 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179689 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186266089 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179689 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186366836 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179689 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186366843 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179689 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186367394 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179689 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186388216 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179689 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186388223 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179689 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186489265 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179689 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding the Curl of a Vector Field Using Linearity Properties" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179689 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186179548 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179689 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "4f429709-cb4b-5319-8d2b-0858bc409e89" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179691 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048813 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179691 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050885 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179691 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186179908 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179691 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186179912 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179691 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186241136 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179691 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186241148 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179691 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186266091 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179691 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186366864 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179691 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186371527 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179691 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186386175 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179691 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186388230 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179691 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186489198 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179691 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186489230 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179691 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186489290 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179691 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding the Curl of a Vector Field Using the Product Rule" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179691 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186179550 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179691 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "ffe539af-1253-5ce3-a65b-eb50ed639c52" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179854 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186180412 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179854 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186180415 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179854 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186180418 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179854 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186180419 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179854 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179854 #edb/ref 1045 "Properties of the Curl Operator" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179854 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186180418 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179854 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186049826 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179854 #edb/ref 1050 720.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179854 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "3c60e1af-3567-58af-98f2-930ea8934370" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179911 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186177135 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179911 #edb/ref 1069 "q-105798" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179911 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179911 #edb/ref 1071 "Calculate $∇\\times (F-G)$ at the point $(-2,0,3)$ for the vector fields $F$ and $G,$ where\n\n$$\nF(x,y,z)=z{e}^{y}\\,i+3\\,j-x\\,k,\\,(∇\\times G)(x,y,z)=2x{e}^{y}\\,i-2{e}^{y}\\,j.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179911 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ffa5f452-f601-5e92-9491-4a18ed51c649" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179911 #edb/ref 1074 "If $F$ and $G$ are vector fields and $k$ is a constant, then we have the following linearity properties for curl:\n\n- $∇\\times (kF)=k∇\\times F$\n\n- $∇\\times (F+G)=∇\\times F+∇\\times G$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\times (F-G) & =∇\\times F-∇\\times G.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to calculate $∇\\times F.$ In doing so, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\times F & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x} & \\frac{∂}{∂y} & \\frac{∂}{∂z} \\\\ z{e}^{y} & 3 & -x\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =[\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-x)-\\frac{∂}{∂z}(3)]i-[\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-x)-\\frac{∂}{∂z}(z{e}^{y})]j+[\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3)-\\frac{∂}{∂y}(z{e}^{y})]k \\\\  & =(0-0)i-((-1)-{e}^{y})j+(0-z{e}^{y})k \\\\  & =({e}^{y}+1)\\,j-z{e}^{y}\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the linearity properties, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\times (F-G) & =∇\\times F-∇\\times G \\\\  & =({e}^{y}+1)\\,j-z{e}^{y}\\,k-[2x{e}^{y}\\,i-2{e}^{y}\\,j] \\\\  & =({e}^{y}+1)\\,j-z{e}^{y}\\,k-2x{e}^{y}\\,i+2{e}^{y}\\,j \\\\  & =-2x{e}^{y}\\,i+(3{e}^{y}+1)\\,j-z{e}^{y}\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating at the point $(-2,0,3),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(∇\\times (F-G))(-2,0,3) & =-2(-2){e}^{0}\\,i+(3{e}^{0}+1)\\,j-3{e}^{0}\\,k \\\\  & =4\\,i+4\\,j-3\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180186 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186178660 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180186 #edb/ref 1069 "q-41898" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180186 #edb/ref 1071 "Calculate $∇ \\cdot (F + 2G)$ at the point $(1, 1, 1)$ for the vector fields $F$ and $G$, where\n\n$F(x, y, z) = x^{2}zi + x^{2}yk, (∇ \\cdot G)(x, y, z) = x^{2}zi + (xy - 2xyz)j - xzk$." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180186 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "4782b4c7-6c0f-5ffd-b7ef-0e4192a99f22" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180412 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186180419 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180412 #edb/ref 1061 32565 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180412 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "e4609d71-fe05-50cf-9a13-ebc4074a2846" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180412 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186179689 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180415 #edb/ref 1061 33332 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180415 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "98443251-1329-5b54-9cf7-7bf7e278e18a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180415 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186179691 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180418 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186180412 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180418 #edb/ref 1061 34361 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180418 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "424de5cf-698c-5da6-94c4-615bbca1376a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180418 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186180687 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180419 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186180415 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180419 #edb/ref 1061 34362 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180419 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "2b40b1da-c024-5f4e-83fe-5a20d7b787c2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180419 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186180688 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180687 #edb/ref 1064 "Recall that if $\\mathbf F = P\\,\\mathbf i + Q\\,\\mathbf j + R\\,\\mathbf k$ is a vector field on $\\mathbb R^3,$ then the curl of $\\mathbf F$ is given by\n\n![](images/ce/ce53aa0d141921708855b14deda9363d69788fd9525bdcbc4426d887aa4d0f70.svg)\n\nThe curl is a quantity that's made up of derivatives, so we would expect it to have similar properties to derivatives.\n\nFor example, if $\\mathbf F$ and $\\mathbf H$ are vector fields and $k$ is a scalar constant, then we have the following linearity properties:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & ∇\\times (kF)=k\\,∇\\times F \\\\  & ∇\\times (F+G)=∇\\times F+∇\\times G\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying these rules can often help us to save time when computing curl. Let's see some examples." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180687 #edb/ref 1065 5520 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180687 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "61a8e799-6b8c-5b9e-b729-92a3f4d062ca" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180687 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180688 #edb/ref 1064 "Recall that for functions $f(x)$ and $g(x),$ the product rule for differentiation is given by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d }{\\textrm d x}(f\\cdot g) = f\\dfrac{\\textrm d }{\\textrm d x}(g) +  g\\dfrac{\\textrm d }{\\textrm d x}(f).\n$$\n\nThere is an analogous rule for computing the curl of the product of a vector field and a scalar field.\n\nSuppose that $\\mathbf F$ is a vector field and $f$ is a scalar field. Then, we have the following identity:\n\n$$\n\\nabla\\times(f\\,\\mathbf F) = f \\, \\nabla \\times \\mathbf {F}+   \\nabla \\! f \\times \\mathbf F\n$$\n\nwhere $\\nabla f$ is the gradient of $f.$\n\nNotice that the del operator $\\nabla$ acts on the terms in the same way as the $\\dfrac{\\textrm d}{\\textrm d x}$ operator in the product rule for differentiation!\n\nThere are a few other tips that we can use to remember this rule:\n\n- The term $\\nabla\\times(f\\,\\mathbf F)$ on the left-hand side must be a vector field because it's a curl. So, the two terms on the right-hand side must be vector fields, too.\n\n- The term $f \\, \\nabla \\times \\mathbf {F}$ on the right-hand side is a vector field because $f$ is a scalar field, $\\nabla \\times \\mathbf F$ is a vector field, and the product of a scalar and a vector field is a vector field. Note that this \"product\" is just regular multiplication.\n\n- The term $\\nabla \\! f \\times \\mathbf F$ on the right-hand side is a vector field because both $\\nabla f$ and $\\mathbf F$ are vector fields, and the *cross-product* of two vector fields is a vector field." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180688 #edb/ref 1065 5521 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180688 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "0eb2c12d-4d29-5fe3-b3a4-864368ada8f3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180688 #edb/ref 1067 "The Product Rule for Curl" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371527 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186371528 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371527 #edb/ref 1069 "q-105895" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371527 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371527 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨-1,0,x{y}^{3}⟩$ and the scalar field $f(x,y,z)=yz.$ Given that\n\n$$\n∇\\times F=⟨3x{y}^{2},-{y}^{3},0⟩,\\,∇f=⟨0,z,y⟩,\n$$\n\ncalculate $∇\\times (f\\,F)$ at the point $(0,-1,2).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371527 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b8abe39b-bb92-55f7-bb84-89cdb536b574" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371527 #edb/ref 1074 "We shall use the following identity:\n\n$$\n∇\\times (f\\,F)=f∇\\times F+∇f\\times F\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute $∇f\\times F.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f\\times F & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 0 & z & y \\\\ -1 & 0 & x{y}^{3}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =[z(x{y}^{3})-y(0)]i-[0(x{y}^{3})-y(-1)]j+[0(0)-z(-1)]k \\\\  & =xz{y}^{3}\\,i-y\\,j+z\\,k \\\\  & =⟨xz{y}^{3},-y,z⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\times (f\\,F) & =f∇\\times F+∇f\\times F \\\\  & =yz⟨3x{y}^{2},-{y}^{3},0⟩+⟨xz{y}^{3},-y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3xz{y}^{3},-z{y}^{4},0⟩+⟨xz{y}^{3},-y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4xz{y}^{3},-z{y}^{4}-y,z⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\times (f\\,F)(0,-1,2) & =⟨4(0)(2)(-1)^{3},-2(-1)^{4}-(-1),2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,-1,2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371528 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186371529 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371528 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186371530 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371528 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186371531 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371528 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186371532 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371528 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186371533 #edb/ref 17592186045417]